WEBVTT


00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.980
I took thousands of perfumes and charted where fragrances were in them.

00:00:03.981 --> 00:00:05.300
This gave me a nice little chart,

00:00:05.301 --> 00:00:07.400
but then I noticed something really interesting.

00:00:07.401 --> 00:00:08.660
So to rewind a little bit,

00:00:08.661 --> 00:00:12.900
I'm a data journalist and I made this project that takes every perfume out there

00:00:12.901 --> 00:00:14.340
and looks up its fragrances.

00:00:14.341 --> 00:00:17.540
You get a visual diagram of what scents go well together

00:00:17.541 --> 00:00:18.960
and make for the best perfume.

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:20.180
Something that I thought was, like,

00:00:20.181 --> 00:00:23.400
super interesting was how all of these perfumes were organised.

00:00:23.460 --> 00:00:27.540
Basically, every scent out there has a time where it evaporates

00:00:27.541 --> 00:00:29.820
and that's what determines the classification.

00:00:29.821 --> 00:00:31.940
A perfume with a lot more base notes,

00:00:31.941 --> 00:00:32.540
for instance,

00:00:32.541 --> 00:00:36.840
will last longer than a perfume with a lot more heart notes or top notes.

00:00:36.980 --> 00:00:40.140
What I do in my chart is group all of those notes together.

00:00:40.141 --> 00:00:43.140
You can kinda see what type of scents make up each category.

00:00:43.141 --> 00:00:45.100
For instance, if you look at the spicy note,

00:00:45.101 --> 00:00:47.580
it's often used as a top, as a finish.

00:00:47.581 --> 00:00:51.060
Probably cause a lot of the spicy scents evaporate quickly.

00:00:51.061 --> 00:00:54.040
If you look at all the fragrances in the heart category,

00:00:54.080 --> 00:00:56.220
not a lot of those scents are spicy.

00:00:56.221 --> 00:00:57.420
And if you look at the base,

00:00:57.421 --> 00:00:59.020
even less so. Either way,

00:00:59.021 --> 00:01:00.460
this world is super fascinating.

00:01:00.461 --> 00:01:02.400
I'm still trying to find my way around it.
